# Patch 0008 — one writer per sidecar: withStoreLock, taken by track-edit, track-comment and track-reply
# Offered back: no (not yet: written as offerable to the engine's author, held back by the standing word of 2026-09-11 to open nothing new upstream)
# Files: store-io.mjs, cli/track-comment.mjs, cli/track-edit.mjs, cli/track-reply.mjs
#
# Reason: every writer of a sidecar loads it, changes the object and renames a temp over it, and two
# writers interleaved between load and rename each save a store that lacks the other's change, so
# the second rename erases the first's write (a reply lost under an editor host's comment, an edit's
# op lost under a reply). Measured against the real CLIs and an editor host, one write in five was
# lost at a stagger of 4 to 20 ms (2026-09-09); a stat before the rename narrows that window and
# cannot close it. store-io gains withStoreLock(storePath, fn): an O_EXCL create of `<sidecar>.lock`
# holding "pid ts", a few milliseconds' sleep and retry for up to STORE_LOCK_WAIT_MS (2 s) when
# held, a StoreLockError past that (held: true) or when the name is taken by a non-lock or the lock
# cannot be created, written, read or removed (held: false, the OS error as its cause), and a break
# of a lock whose writer is dead or whose stamp is more than STORE_LOCK_STALE_MS (15 s) from the
# reader's clock, behind or ahead (judged by age behind alone, a lock committed or planted with a
# stamp in the future held every writer of its file to a refusal for as long as it stayed there).
# Breaking has one winner: the breakers serialize on a second O_EXCL name beside the lock,
# `<sidecar>.lock.break`, and the one holding that claim judges the lock again before unlinking it,
# so two waiters that read one dead lock together cannot both remove it and the second take away the
# first's fresh lock (judged and removed by name alone, two of three writers arriving on a dead lock
# both wrote in 54 rounds of 100, 2026-09-11). The judged lock, and a dead claim, stay open on a
# descriptor from the judgment through the unlink: an inode with a descriptor open is not freed, so
# the filesystem cannot give its number to a fresh lock at the name in between, and the breaker
# cannot remove that fresh lock in the dead one's place (compared by number alone, a breaker
# suspended past the bound between its claim check and its look did just that, 2026-09-11, the
# review). Release, in finally, unlinks the lock only while the entry at its name is still the inode
# this holder created (the fd stays open for the hold), so a live writer broken as stale does not
# remove the breaker's lock on its way out; a stamp that cannot be written after the create closes
# the fd, unlinks the empty lock and fails, rather than leaving a lock judged by its age alone.
#
# The `.trackchanges/` folder is made for the lock when missing (a first write in a fresh vault) and
# removed again by the writer that made it when nothing else landed in it, so a refused write leaves
# no trace; when another writer's lock is in the folder at that moment, the removal passes to that
# lock's holder by a "made-dir" line appended to it (or to a breaker's claim), honored at release
# and inherited by a breaker when the holder dies. An entry at the folder's name that is not a
# directory (a dangling link, a link to a file, a plain file) is a lock that cannot be created:
# refused at once, held false, with an error naming the entry, instead of retried until the wait
# runs out and reported as a live writer.
#
# A stamped pid is judged by kill(pid, 0) only by a reader in the pid namespace that stamped it.
# Pids are per namespace: from a child pid namespace (a sandboxed tool shell) every process outside
# is ESRCH, alive or not, and a pid stamped inside one names, outside it, whatever process has that
# number there; judged by pid regardless, a CLI in such a sandbox read an editor host's live lock as
# a dead writer's and broke it at once, and the host broke the sandboxed CLI's live lock whenever
# its pid was a free number outside (2026-09-11, the review). So a writer outside the initial
# namespace (the inode of /proc/self/ns/pid, compared with the initial namespace's; a process that
# cannot read the link is taken to be in it) names its own on a second stamp line, `ns <inode>`, and
# a reader judges the pid only when the lock names the reader's own namespace; any other lock is
# judged by its stamp's age alone.
#
# Not reentrant. The three CLIs take the lock around their load-to-rename (track-edit: the file read
# through the file write and the thread turn; track-comment and track-reply: the note read through
# the save), printing "another editor is writing this file; retry" and exiting 1 when it is not
# obtained. Their fail() now throws a CliFailure the entry point prints and exits 1 on, instead of
# exiting on the spot, so a lock held at that moment is released (process.exit skips every finally);
# the exit status and the message are unchanged. The exported pure functions keep their signatures.
#
# Tests: tools/store-io-lock.test.mjs (the lock's rules, with real-process races),
# tools/store-io-lock-inode-reuse.test.mjs (the judged inode kept open through the unlink),
# tools/store-io-lock-folder-name.test.mjs (a non-directory at the folder's name),
# tools/store-io-lock-pid-namespace.test.mjs (the pid judged only by a reader in the namespace that
# stamped it), tools/store-io-lock-clock.test.mjs (a stamp ahead of the reader's clock),
# tools/vendor-patches.test.mjs (P8 cases) and tools/file-comments-host-store-lock.test.mjs (the
# CLIs waiting for a held lock).
